00:03:57.960 --> 00:04:01.560
Brian is also author of the
book R for

00:04:01.560 --> 00:04:02.730
Actuaries
and Data

00:04:02.730 --> 00:04:05.560
Scientists, with
applications to insurance.

00:04:05.560 --> 00:04:09.030
So really excited to ask
you about that today too, Brian.

00:04:09.030 --> 00:04:10.800
But to get us started here,

00:04:10.800 --> 00:04:12.510
it'd be great to have
you introduce yourself,

00:04:12.510 --> 00:04:15.690
and maybe share a little
bit about your role now,

00:04:15.690 --> 00:04:19.260
and thing you like to
do outside of work too OK.

00:04:19.260 --> 00:04:23.040
- So I'm an actuary and
have been doing that for--

00:04:23.040 --> 00:04:25.380
we're almost at the point
where I can say 30 years.

00:04:25.380 --> 00:04:28.620
I rounding up slightly
to get to 30 years, but.

00:04:28.620 --> 00:04:30.900
I've been doing
that for a while

00:04:30.900 --> 00:04:33.840
one of the first questions that
I get when I tell people that

00:04:33.840 --> 00:04:37.860
I'm an actuary is what
exactly is an actuary?

00:04:37.860 --> 00:04:40.530
I'll keep it very, very
simple for folks, though,

00:04:40.530 --> 00:04:44.720
I'm happy to go into detail
if folks are interested.

00:04:44.720 --> 00:04:48.170
An actuary is
an applied statistician particularly

00:04:48.170 --> 00:04:50.570
focused on financial risk.

00:04:50.570 --> 00:04:51.740
The

00:04:51.740 --> 00:04:54.470
easier way to think
of that perhaps is

00:04:54.470 --> 00:04:56.570
we work a lot in insurance.

00:04:56.570 --> 00:05:00.710
We figure out what people
need to pay for transferring the

00:05:00.710 --> 00:05:03.560
risk of financial loss to
damage to their car, to their

00:05:03.560 --> 00:05:08.360
home, or to what have you--
any number of things. So that's

00:05:08.360 --> 00:05:10.380
what an actuary does.

00:05:10.380 --> 00:05:13.040
I've been with the
Casualty Actuarial Society for about

00:05:13.040 --> 00:05:19.160
five years-- more than five
years. And we are very engaged

00:05:19.160 --> 00:05:20.750
in professional education,

00:05:20.750 --> 00:05:24.840
credentialing actuaries but
also actuarial research.

00:05:24.840 --> 00:05:29.300
So that's where I nerded
up with trying to develop

00:05:29.300 --> 00:05:33.050
new concepts to improve

00:05:33.050 --> 00:05:36.530
the way that actuaries
can carry out their work.

00:05:36.530 --> 00:05:38.960
I also do a lot of
speaking. As I mentioned I,

00:05:38.960 --> 00:05:43.490
was just in Boston recently at
a conference, so I do dabble

00:05:43.490 --> 00:05:46.040
somewhat in
professional education.

00:05:46.040 --> 00:05:50.660
Outside of work, it
will be the same set of

00:05:50.660 --> 00:05:53.600
nonprofessional hobbies that I
think everyone does that are

00:05:53.600 --> 00:05:55.850
fascinating and
enriching to me.

00:05:55.850 --> 00:06:00.830
I spend time with my wife and
my kids. I cook. I watch movies.

00:06:00.830 --> 00:06:04.100
I can't think of anything
that would really distinguish me all

00:06:04.100 --> 00:06:08.990
that much outside of work. I
like to travel, but we all do.

00:06:08.990 --> 00:06:11.030
It's a really dreadful
dating profile,

00:06:11.030 --> 00:06:15.350
but I'm happy to talk
about movies or travel [INAUDIBLE].

00:06:15.350 --> 00:06:18.140
- Well, thank you so much
for joining us, Brian.

00:06:18.140 --> 00:06:20.390
And I just shared the link
to the book in the chat,

00:06:20.390 --> 00:06:22.880
if anybody wants
to check it out.

00:06:22.880 --> 00:06:23.330
But, Brian,

00:06:23.330 --> 00:06:25.640
something I thought might
be interesting to kick-off the

00:06:25.640 --> 00:06:27.710
conversation with is--

00:06:27.710 --> 00:06:33.290
I know you mentioned that a
majority of actuaries are still doing

00:06:33.290 --> 00:06:39.620
the lion's share of their
work in spreadsheets, specifically Excel.

00:06:39.620 --> 00:06:42.950
But I was just wondering how
you've seen that start to

00:06:42.950 --> 00:06:47.230
change or how you think that
might be changing in the future?

00:06:47.230 --> 00:06:50.160
- So it is changing,
albeit slowly.

00:06:50.160 --> 00:06:53.820
And I can say that my
experience professionally as an

00:06:53.820 --> 00:06:59.460
actuary for, oh, the first 20
years that I was doing this was

00:06:59.460 --> 00:07:03.100
largely spreadsheet-based. That's
very, very common.

00:07:03.100 --> 00:07:05.130
There's a very

00:07:05.130 --> 00:07:09.300
easy on ramp for people
to work with Excel,

00:07:09.300 --> 00:07:11.610
which I think helps explain
some of its ubiquity.

00:07:11.610 --> 00:07:13.200
And I'll say Excel,

00:07:13.200 --> 00:07:17.490
that's usually what I
mean-- the spreadsheet more generally.

00:07:17.490 --> 00:07:19.490
But typically it's
going to be Excel.

00:07:21.330 --> 00:07:25.710
Two years ago, the CAS

00:07:25.710 --> 00:07:29.250
issued a technology survey
about what tools actuaries were

00:07:29.250 --> 00:07:33.420
using because we're curious to
see how quickly it's moving.

00:07:33.420 --> 00:07:36.120
What we found was 98%

00:07:36.120 --> 00:07:38.790
of respondents-- and we
got about 1,000-some-odd

00:07:38.790 --> 00:07:42.000
respondents, so a pretty
decent sample size--

00:07:42.000 --> 00:07:44.040
said the used-- 98%,

00:07:44.040 --> 00:07:48.360
I think, use Excel on
pretty much a daily basis,

00:07:48.360 --> 00:07:52.680
whereas R or Python,
the numbers were

00:07:52.680 --> 00:07:55.290
on a daily basis certainly
comfortably less than one out

00:07:55.290 --> 00:08:00.450
of three. I would be one of
the ones who uses R every day.

00:08:00.450 --> 00:08:04.230
We redid the survey last year
and the numbers moved a little

00:08:04.230 --> 00:08:06.840
bit. That survey
will launch again

00:08:06.840 --> 00:08:12.600
this summer and curious to see
if that slow and steady pace

00:08:12.600 --> 00:08:17.040
is continuing. But,
anecdotally, I can say that

00:08:17.040 --> 00:08:19.620
when I'm at conferences and

00:08:19.620 --> 00:08:21.450
talking about some of the work,

00:08:21.450 --> 00:08:26.820
I do talk to folks about what
tools they are using. I hear more

00:08:26.820 --> 00:08:29.250
of a-- Python is moving
a little bit quicker,

00:08:29.250 --> 00:08:31.730
largely because of R

00:08:31.730 --> 00:08:33.750
had some penetration.

00:08:33.750 --> 00:08:37.140
But I do get the sense that

00:08:37.140 --> 00:08:39.000
there is a subtle shift.

00:08:39.000 --> 00:08:41.370
A lot of it is going
to be demographic.

00:08:41.370 --> 00:08:43.260
Within the actuarial community,

00:08:43.260 --> 00:08:46.560
I'll be one of
the older R users.

00:08:46.560 --> 00:08:48.970
They tend to be a little
bit younger than I am,

00:08:48.970 --> 00:08:53.220
so that shift is partially
a generational change.

00:08:57.310 --> 00:09:01.650
- And with that, have
you had experience in

00:09:01.650 --> 00:09:03.030
helping bring people on board?

00:09:03.030 --> 00:09:06.870
I'm curious what's been most
helpful for people just getting started.

00:09:06.870 --> 00:09:07.020
-

00:09:07.020 --> 00:09:10.350
I'm not sure what has or
hasn't been helpful for other people.

00:09:10.350 --> 00:09:13.320
I can tell you my
approach has been to

00:09:13.320 --> 00:09:15.840
zealously evangelize

00:09:15.840 --> 00:09:18.810
using scripting tools.
I'll say R specifically.

00:09:18.810 --> 00:09:22.140
It's the one that I came to
first and the one that I love

00:09:22.140 --> 00:09:25.320
the most, but
Python is good too.

00:09:25.320 --> 00:09:29.400
I have less to say about
the things like Julia or JavaScript--

00:09:29.400 --> 00:09:30.990
JavaScript might not
be a great example,

00:09:30.990 --> 00:09:33.390
but Julia would be a good
one. They're not bad,

00:09:33.390 --> 00:09:35.080
I'm just not as
familiar with them.

00:09:35.080 --> 00:09:38.220
And I think they don't
have quite the network effect.

00:09:38.220 --> 00:09:39.840
But

00:09:39.840 --> 00:09:43.600
evangelizing scripting tools is one
of the things that I do.

00:09:43.600 --> 00:09:46.140
That's one of the reasons why
I wrote the book so that there

00:09:46.140 --> 00:09:50.160
would be an entry
point for folks to

00:09:50.160 --> 00:09:52.890
get familiar with the language.

00:09:52.890 --> 00:09:57.180
Later today, I'll be
constructing a virtual workshop

00:09:57.180 --> 00:10:02.970
for actuaries using R. And I
also assist with the Python instruction.

00:10:02.970 --> 00:10:05.350
I hope that's helpful.

00:10:05.350 --> 00:10:08.070
I can tell you what has
been helpful for me is just

00:10:08.070 --> 00:10:11.610
recognizing the
benefit that I get from

00:10:11.610 --> 00:10:14.130
using technologies
like R, like Python.

00:10:14.130 --> 00:10:15.690
It has made me

00:10:15.690 --> 00:10:17.670
a better statistician.

00:10:17.670 --> 00:10:22.650
I suddenly found that when I was
able to script a lot of these models,

00:10:22.650 --> 00:10:25.680
I understood the statistics a lot
better than I thought I did

00:10:25.680 --> 00:10:30.060
when I was trying to
translate pen and paper into

00:10:30.060 --> 00:10:32.060
a spreadsheet.

00:10:32.970 --> 00:10:33.930
- Thank you.

00:10:33.930 --> 00:10:36.480
So I get to ask all the
questions in the beginning as

00:10:36.480 --> 00:10:39.000
we wait for them to
come in from everybody,

00:10:39.000 --> 00:10:43.020
but something I find
really interesting is learning about

00:10:43.020 --> 00:10:44.760
people's professional journeys,

00:10:44.760 --> 00:10:46.920
and what takes you to
different companies,

00:10:46.920 --> 00:10:49.140
and gets you to where you are.

00:10:49.140 --> 00:10:52.200
And I know you've worked
across many different insurance

00:10:52.200 --> 00:10:56.430
companies, like Munich Re
or Swiss Reinsurance. You were

00:10:56.430 --> 00:10:59.230
chief actuary as well.

00:10:59.230 --> 00:11:01.440
And I'd love to hear

00:11:01.440 --> 00:11:04.740
about that journey and maybe
also what that transition has

00:11:04.740 --> 00:11:07.050
been like going into this--

00:11:07.050 --> 00:11:08.850
the Casualty Actuarial

00:11:08.850 --> 00:11:11.880
Society, more of a
professional society

00:11:11.880 --> 00:11:15.780
rather than a
large organization.

00:11:15.780 --> 00:11:16.020
- Well,

00:11:16.020 --> 00:11:21.390
I did say that I love to travel
and the ways that I tried to put that

00:11:21.390 --> 00:11:22.620
into

00:11:22.620 --> 00:11:26.850
action was I was
working in Chicago--

00:11:26.850 --> 00:11:30.810
this would have been about
20 years ago now-- and

00:11:30.810 --> 00:11:33.720
one of the professional
realities of white collar work

00:11:33.720 --> 00:11:36.540
that some of us have
experienced, unfortunately,

00:11:36.540 --> 00:11:39.060
is company where I was working

00:11:39.060 --> 00:11:41.740
was sold to someone else
and so I thought, well,

00:11:41.740 --> 00:11:43.740
I'd like to work

00:11:45.060 --> 00:11:47.130
somewhere outside of
the United States.

00:11:47.130 --> 00:11:50.070
So I have this opportunity
where I find myself

00:11:50.070 --> 00:11:52.230
open to other

00:11:52.230 --> 00:11:54.600
positions and

00:11:54.600 --> 00:11:57.600
was able to move to Germany,

00:11:57.600 --> 00:12:00.750
not because of I had said
that's going to be the place,

00:12:00.750 --> 00:12:04.140
I phones a headhunter and

00:12:04.140 --> 00:12:07.950
said, I'd like to
move somewhere on Earth where

00:12:07.950 --> 00:12:10.590
English is not the
native language.

00:12:10.590 --> 00:12:15.480
Within this particular field that
means-- if you want to be

00:12:15.480 --> 00:12:18.360
a non-native-- or rather
English is not the native

00:12:18.360 --> 00:12:21.300
language, that will be either
Switzerland, or Germany,

00:12:21.300 --> 00:12:24.570
maybe France, maybe a
few other countries.

00:12:24.570 --> 00:12:28.110
But that was where I
wound up. It was terrific

00:12:28.110 --> 00:12:30.960
learning German, got
to travel quite a bit,

00:12:30.960 --> 00:12:35.250
and got to see a very
different perspective.

00:12:35.250 --> 00:12:36.030
I know that I'm--

00:12:36.030 --> 00:12:39.060
I'm pretty sure that I'm
the only actuary on this call--

00:12:39.060 --> 00:12:42.210
although if you're
not, please say hello.

00:12:42.210 --> 00:12:44.940
So for people that are
not familiar with the field,

00:12:44.940 --> 00:12:50.160
the perspective and the mode of
practice is different all over the world.

00:12:50.160 --> 00:12:52.800
Actuaries in London are
different than actuaries in New

00:12:52.800 --> 00:12:55.290
York, are different
than actuaries in Asia,

00:12:55.290 --> 00:12:58.950
are different than actuaries
in Switzerland. So getting that

00:12:58.950 --> 00:13:00.960
perspective was

00:13:00.960 --> 00:13:02.970
really eye-opening.

00:13:02.970 --> 00:13:04.350
Also,

00:13:04.350 --> 00:13:09.486
fed into my interest
in R because the--

00:13:09.486 --> 00:13:12.240
a lot of the folks that
I've worked with in Europe,

00:13:12.240 --> 00:13:17.470
their statistical skills
were loads better than mine.

00:13:17.470 --> 00:13:21.120
I think they're able to think
in equations and be able to do

00:13:21.120 --> 00:13:26.790
things in ways that I struggle to
keep up with. So that fed into

00:13:26.790 --> 00:13:27.990
my chafing

00:13:27.990 --> 00:13:31.410
against the constraints
of the spreadsheet and

00:13:31.410 --> 00:13:32.790
I'm going to say
my own intellect.

00:13:32.790 --> 00:13:37.500
I'll go ahead and just out myself
with that. Like I say, I know

00:13:37.500 --> 00:13:40.380
more mathematical statistics than
the average person on the

00:13:40.380 --> 00:13:44.400
street, but I don't know as
