00:17:23.040 --> 00:17:25.040
- There are a few.

00:17:26.609 --> 00:17:31.560
And I'll have to
reference some specific actuarial

00:17:31.560 --> 00:17:34.950
techniques. There
is a technique

00:17:34.950 --> 00:17:37.500
called loss reserving.
Effectively,

00:17:37.500 --> 00:17:39.930
it's establishing the
amount of liability--

00:17:39.930 --> 00:17:41.250
financial liability--

00:17:41.250 --> 00:17:43.320
and I'll get back to the
boring stuff in a minute--

00:17:43.320 --> 00:17:47.250
but financial liability
for insurance company.

00:17:47.250 --> 00:17:48.900
Practically, statistically,

00:17:48.900 --> 00:17:52.590
it is very similar to
longitudinal data analysis, not

00:17:52.590 --> 00:17:55.260
quite the same, but
very, very similar.

00:17:55.260 --> 00:18:00.480
That is one area where
there are at least two packages,

00:18:00.480 --> 00:18:02.910
both of them are
called chain ladder.

00:18:02.910 --> 00:18:07.110
One of them is an R and
then there was a port to Python.

00:18:07.110 --> 00:18:08.700
Those are a couple.

00:18:08.700 --> 00:18:12.660
There's a package called
Actuar, A-C-T-U-A-R,

00:18:12.660 --> 00:18:15.630
which is I think of
French for actuary,

00:18:15.630 --> 00:18:17.640
developed in France.

00:18:17.640 --> 00:18:22.050
This is some
support for statistical distributions

00:18:22.050 --> 00:18:26.520
beyond what you get
in the stats package.

00:18:26.520 --> 00:18:28.410
Actuaries

00:18:28.410 --> 00:18:32.220
often need to rely on
probability distributions

00:18:32.220 --> 00:18:34.740
better-- that have
thick tails, all right?

00:18:34.740 --> 00:18:37.290
So we've got highly skewed

00:18:37.290 --> 00:18:40.350
distributions. This is a good
thing if you're a consumer.

00:18:40.350 --> 00:18:43.680
It means that really
terrible things are rare--

00:18:43.680 --> 00:18:46.410
really terrible
events are rare.

00:18:46.410 --> 00:18:49.680
But as actuaries we need to be
able to come up with an estimate there.

00:18:49.680 --> 00:18:52.530
So Actuar is another package.

00:18:52.530 --> 00:18:55.200
I'm searching my memory.

00:18:55.200 --> 00:18:57.990
That maybe about it,

00:18:57.990 --> 00:18:59.610
