actuary, so I'm talking out
of school a little bit.

00:27:34.700 --> 00:27:37.850
But I think there are a
lot of techniques in survival analysis

00:27:37.850 --> 00:27:42.050
that have their origin in
human mortality studies.

00:27:42.050 --> 00:27:45.920
So def-- yeah,
survival analysis is

00:27:45.920 --> 00:27:51.200
a thing. I wrote a paper
about using survival analysis in loss reserving,

00:27:51.200 --> 00:27:54.650
so you could think of that
as a hybrid survival analysis and

00:27:54.650 --> 00:27:56.810
longitudinal analysis.

00:27:59.860 --> 00:28:05.200
- I didn't Google that
paper fast enough down there.

00:28:05.200 --> 00:28:07.930
- There are better
sources out there.

00:28:07.930 --> 00:28:10.870
- So, OK, maybe taking

00:28:10.870 --> 00:28:13.780
a step back from some of the

00:28:13.780 --> 00:28:14.920
insurance topics--

00:28:14.920 --> 00:28:18.220
I know you said that you do
a lot of public speaking and

00:28:18.220 --> 00:28:22.240
that's something that has come up as
a question before here in the hangout.

00:28:22.240 --> 00:28:26.470
Do you have any tips for us who
are trying to get better at public speaking?

00:28:31.020 --> 00:28:32.820
- I think

00:28:32.820 --> 00:28:38.470
finding a friendly room is
not a bad place to start.

00:28:38.470 --> 00:28:42.090
So, locally, here in Research
Triangle, North Carolina,

00:28:42.090 --> 00:28:43.770
there are not very
many actuaries.

00:28:43.770 --> 00:28:47.700
But I think folks realize that
this is a really good spot to

00:28:47.700 --> 00:28:50.220
be if you're into data
science or statistics.

00:28:50.220 --> 00:28:52.380
We've got the Research
Triangle Park,

00:28:52.380 --> 00:28:55.720
we've got the universities,
and we've got [? SaaS ?] here.

00:28:55.720 --> 00:29:00.390
So there is a very
active and super friendly community of

00:29:00.390 --> 00:29:01.800
data scientists.

00:29:01.800 --> 00:29:03.450
I'm

00:29:03.450 --> 00:29:06.750
very much involved with
a meetup group of Research

00:29:06.750 --> 00:29:08.460
Triangle analysts.

00:29:08.460 --> 00:29:10.020
And

00:29:10.020 --> 00:29:13.920
we have monthly meetings.
People can give talks there.

00:29:13.920 --> 00:29:15.930
Really friendly room.

00:29:15.930 --> 00:29:19.000
That's not a bad
place to start.

00:29:19.000 --> 00:29:21.516
What else can I say?

00:29:21.516 --> 00:29:24.990
Yeah, present to your family,
present to your friends.

00:29:24.990 --> 00:29:27.570
That's another way to
find a friendly room.

00:29:27.570 --> 00:29:30.300
And then, finally,
just keep doing it.

00:29:30.300 --> 00:29:34.550
The first time that I
ever spoke at an actuarial conference,

00:29:34.550 --> 00:29:35.790
I

00:29:35.790 --> 00:29:39.430
was so nervous.

00:29:39.430 --> 00:29:43.620
I thought that I was going
to have a heart attack. I

00:29:43.620 --> 00:29:45.200
couldn't

00:29:45.200 --> 00:29:48.080
believe just how
petrified I was.

00:29:48.080 --> 00:29:52.775
But that was-- what was that? That
was 13, 14 years ago. It gets easier.

00:29:55.860 --> 00:29:55.900
-

00:29:55.900 --> 00:29:58.550
I love that tip of
just reaching out to different

00:29:58.550 --> 00:30:01.580
meetups groups and speaking there too.

00:30:01.580 --> 00:30:05.950
And I wanted to call out that
Abigail just put in the chat because-- well,

00:30:05.950 --> 00:30:09.320
we were talking a little
bit earlier about this on LinkedIn.

00:30:09.320 --> 00:30:12.140
But she was looking for
lightning talks for a DC

00:30:12.140 --> 00:30:16.340
