to share in the chat,

00:30:17.940 --> 00:30:20.480
that would be great too.

00:30:20.480 --> 00:30:23.600
- Yeah, pretty much any
major area is going to have--

00:30:23.600 --> 00:30:26.840
I've not managed to get
any of the DC meetups.

00:30:26.840 --> 00:30:31.070
But DC area is kind of
where home office is. So when I'm

00:30:31.070 --> 00:30:34.220
going to the CAS
office, it's in DC.

00:30:34.220 --> 00:30:36.920
And I keep trying to catch
up with the DC folks.

00:30:36.920 --> 00:30:40.310
But if you're
in Boston, Chicago, Philadelphia,

00:30:40.310 --> 00:30:43.430
there's going to be a data
nerd meetup in your town.

00:30:45.830 --> 00:30:47.650
- I can vouch for Boston.

00:30:47.650 --> 00:30:50.630
That's our group. [CHUCKLES]

00:30:50.630 --> 00:30:52.530
I see, Bill, you had
another question in chat.

00:30:52.530 --> 00:30:54.330
I'll read it here.
But Bill said,

00:30:54.330 --> 00:30:57.410
"I'm always interested in
security and regulatory issues.

00:30:57.410 --> 00:31:01.640
Do you anonymize or make
up fake data? If so, what

00:31:01.640 --> 00:31:03.650
packages or methods
do you use?"

00:31:03.650 --> 00:31:05.650
- Mm-hmm.

00:31:06.350 --> 00:31:10.700
In the insurance space,
there is a need for

00:31:10.700 --> 00:31:15.200
publicly available data sets for
research. As you can guess,

00:31:15.200 --> 00:31:19.010
there are a lot of privacy
issues that would make people

00:31:19.010 --> 00:31:22.130
uncomfortable, companies
certainly, and policyholders,

00:31:22.130 --> 00:31:23.970
very uncomfortable
sharing that data.

00:31:23.970 --> 00:31:28.190
There are a few, kind of,
toy data sets out there.

00:31:28.190 --> 00:31:32.615
But we do simulate data for
research purposes from time to time.

00:31:32.615 --> 00:31:36.420
When I'm researching--
well, two things.

00:31:36.420 --> 00:31:37.550
One,

00:31:37.550 --> 00:31:39.890
again, for that

00:31:39.890 --> 00:31:42.890
reserve data, the
longitudinal-ish data,

00:31:42.890 --> 00:31:45.740
there are several packages
that will do that.

00:31:45.740 --> 00:31:48.230
There are about four or five.

00:31:48.230 --> 00:31:50.690
If you're not an expert,
you've never heard of these things.

00:31:50.690 --> 00:31:53.480
But that will simulate
these kinds of financial

00:31:53.480 --> 00:31:55.865
transactions.

00:31:55.865 --> 00:31:57.280
Beyond that,

00:31:57.280 --> 00:32:00.620
fairly straightforward. The

00:32:00.620 --> 00:32:03.950
ability to randomly
generate a number in R,

00:32:03.950 --> 00:32:07.925
it's one of those
really bedrock things

00:32:07.925 --> 00:32:11.270
that I really enjoyed
when I first started

00:32:11.270 --> 00:32:14.220
using R. In Excel,
I can remember,

00:32:14.220 --> 00:32:17.870
I wanted to simulate
something from a Poisson distribution.

00:32:17.870 --> 00:32:23.270
At that time, probably still today,
there's no easy way to simulate

00:32:23.270 --> 00:32:27.170
a Poisson, which is a
very, very common thing in actuarial

00:32:27.170 --> 00:32:29.250
applications. So
you couldn't do it.

00:32:29.250 --> 00:32:32.090
So I'm reading numerical
recipes and all these other

00:32:32.090 --> 00:32:33.930
crazy things to--

00:32:33.930 --> 00:32:37.466
I think maybe I
could've just inverted

00:32:37.466 --> 00:32:39.930
the cumulative function,
but whatever. In R,

00:32:39.930 --> 00:32:42.110
I don't even need to do
any of that. It's just

00:32:42.110 --> 00:32:47.070
[INAUDIBLE], and I've
got 10,000 observations,

00:32:47.070 --> 00:32:48.545
so things like that.

00:32:48.545 --> 00:32:51.650
And you can stack them.
So if you've got, like,

00:32:51.650 --> 00:32:53.390
a cascading process,

00:32:53.390 --> 00:32:56.285
a sort of a
hierarchical simulation,

00:32:56.285 --> 00:32:59.150
pretty straightforward
to do in R.

00:32:59.150 --> 00:33:02.690
My favorite way
to tackle that is

00:33:02.690 --> 00:33:05.810
I'm a very dataframe-centric
programmer.

00:33:05.810 --> 00:33:09.560
So I've got a data frame
where I've got maybe 1,000 scenarios

00:33:09.560 --> 00:33:13.820
that have different parameters,
and then just go row by row

00:33:13.820 --> 00:33:16.310
using Per or
something like that.

00:33:16.310 --> 00:33:20.120
Each row in a dataframe becomes
a new data frame that has a

00:33:20.120 --> 00:33:21.890
simulating a scenario.

00:33:21.890 --> 00:33:23.210
I can just

00:33:23.210 --> 00:33:26.570
easily nest, and
un-nest, and summarize

00:33:26.570 --> 00:33:28.910
all of that stuff. So

00:33:28.910 --> 00:33:32.930
it's that LEGO-based approach to simulation.

00:33:35.135 --> 00:33:36.980
- Great, thank you.

00:33:36.980 --> 00:33:40.580
Maybe this is a question
for everybody on the call here,

00:33:40.580 --> 00:33:43.280
too, if you want to share
links in the chat. But, Gaurav,

00:33:43.280 --> 00:33:45.280
do you want to jump in here?

00:33:49.190 --> 00:33:52.204
The question was are there

00:33:52.204 --> 00:33:55.610
publicly available data
sets for casualty insurance?

00:33:59.990 --> 00:34:02.180
- So I could've name
checked a few there.

00:34:02.180 --> 00:34:06.116
There's the insurance
data package in R.

00:34:06.116 --> 00:34:07.080
- OK.

00:34:07.080 --> 00:34:07.530
BRIAN FANNIN: Yeah.

00:34:07.530 --> 00:34:10.649
And there's another one that's not
on Cran because it's pretty massive.

00:34:13.440 --> 00:34:15.150
I'll dig up a link to that.

00:34:15.150 --> 00:34:16.679
- Yeah. You can send
to to me after too.

00:34:16.679 --> 00:34:17.489
BRIAN FANNIN: It's all
set to go. Thank you.

00:34:17.489 --> 00:34:19.320
- Yeah.

00:34:19.320 --> 00:34:22.949
I'll try and make notes
of where to follow up.

00:34:22.949 --> 00:34:24.989
Javier, I see you had
a question in the chat too.

00:34:24.989 --> 00:34:26.989
You want to jump in here next?

00:34:29.590 --> 00:34:31.590
JAVIER: Thanks, Rachael.

00:34:32.560 --> 00:34:35.889
Yeah, thanks, Brian. I'm
really enjoying your discussion here.

00:34:35.889 --> 00:34:38.860
I work with a ton of actuaries.
I'm not an actuary myself.

00:34:38.860 --> 00:34:42.429
But I work at a health
care insurance company. So

00:34:42.429 --> 00:34:45.070
I'm in our data Science
Center of Excellence.

00:34:45.070 --> 00:34:48.190
And a lot of our
