00:36:47.715 --> 00:36:50.980
OK, another anonymous
question on Slido was,

00:36:50.980 --> 00:36:53.560
"Do you see R markdown

00:36:53.560 --> 00:36:56.995
or Quarto's publishing
functionality used in actuary

00:36:56.995 --> 00:37:00.280
stats work, for example,
publishing your notebooks into

00:37:00.280 --> 00:37:03.250
tables, docs, or
slides directly?"

00:37:03.250 --> 00:37:05.510
- Not as often as I would like.

00:37:05.510 --> 00:37:10.630
I am a big, big
fan of R Markdown slash Quarto.

00:37:10.630 --> 00:37:12.820
I'm a little bit late
to the party in Quarto.

00:37:12.820 --> 00:37:14.780
i use it.

00:37:14.780 --> 00:37:17.440
The differences between
Quarto and R Markdown,

00:37:17.440 --> 00:37:19.240
I'm still learning. So
I kind of use the terms

00:37:19.240 --> 00:37:21.085
interchangeably.

00:37:21.085 --> 00:37:25.210
I wrote a 600-page
textbook using R Markdown.

00:37:25.210 --> 00:37:29.320
I do pretty much all of
my work in R markdown.

00:37:29.320 --> 00:37:33.370
I find that I'm
writing notes. And

00:37:33.370 --> 00:37:36.760
long-form comments suddenly become
the most important bit

00:37:36.760 --> 00:37:42.100
in the document. And so it
just becomes easy to have R Markdown

00:37:42.100 --> 00:37:45.460
and slash Quarto as my
starting point for work.

00:37:45.460 --> 00:37:47.965
Professionally,
I've used it a lot.

00:37:50.080 --> 00:37:53.890
Stakeholders very
often will want--

00:37:53.890 --> 00:37:56.080
this isn't all that they
want-- but very often,

00:37:56.080 --> 00:37:59.170
they can be satisfied with
a reasonably concise and

00:37:59.170 --> 00:38:02.290
informative Word
document or PDF,

00:38:02.290 --> 00:38:05.170
if you'd rather. But giving
them a Word document, like, OK,

00:38:05.170 --> 00:38:07.930
here you go, here are
the tables, and here are the

00:38:07.930 --> 00:38:11.050
graphs, and here's
the executive summary,

00:38:11.050 --> 00:38:14.430
I think that's a great
way to share information.

00:38:14.430 --> 00:38:16.020
Actuaries are not
using it as much.

00:38:16.020 --> 00:38:19.230
I think there's not
as much awareness

00:38:19.230 --> 00:38:21.360
of R Markdown.

00:38:21.360 --> 00:38:25.950
I think the initial presumption
is that R's greatest strength,

00:38:25.950 --> 00:38:29.250
or only strength, rather,
is in calculation.

00:38:29.250 --> 00:38:32.910
It can absolutely do that.
It's got an incredible library of

00:38:32.910 --> 00:38:36.180
statistical and
machine learning functionality.

00:38:36.180 --> 00:38:38.790
But it does a lot
more than that.

00:38:38.790 --> 00:38:41.970
That is slowly changing,

00:38:41.970 --> 00:38:45.660
as with adoption
of R generally.

00:38:45.660 --> 00:38:49.920
I see that as being a real
big selling point. When people

00:38:49.920 --> 00:38:55.440
find out that they can replace
a very typical actuarial

00:38:55.440 --> 00:39:00.300
and analytic workflow where I create
a plot in Excel and I

00:39:00.300 --> 00:39:04.890
have to copy and paste it into
Word. That's operationally

00:39:04.890 --> 00:39:07.960
inefficient. It's
operationally dangerous.

00:39:07.960 --> 00:39:08.895
So

00:39:08.895 --> 00:39:14.790
the notion that my entire
analytics report is in one file,

00:39:14.790 --> 00:39:17.670
all of the code, all of
the narrative, all of the tables,

00:39:19.785 --> 00:39:23.955
it's like telling me that I
can breathe underwater and fly.

00:39:23.955 --> 00:39:25.270
It's really fantastic.

00:39:25.270 --> 00:39:29.280
So I think that as actuaries
become aware of that

00:39:29.280 --> 00:39:33.030
capability, they will get
pretty happy about it,

00:39:33.030 --> 00:39:36.510
as will the people who
need to communicate with actuaries.

00:39:38.910 --> 00:39:40.290
- I like the way you put that,

00:39:40.290 --> 00:39:44.190
copying and pasting
is operationally dangerous.

00:39:44.190 --> 00:39:45.860
- Yeah.

00:39:45.860 --> 00:39:47.220
Yep.

00:39:47.220 --> 00:39:49.710
I'm not the only actuary
who has been burned by that.

00:39:49.710 --> 00:39:50.535
Where you're in a meeting,

00:39:50.535 --> 00:39:54.480
you have everyone's looking
at the same file. And

00:39:54.480 --> 00:39:56.590
the plot didn't update.

00:39:56.590 --> 00:40:00.190
What do you mean we're losing
$1 billion a day? Oh, sorry,

00:40:00.190 --> 00:40:02.190
that's yesterday's
graph. We're actually--

00:40:05.120 --> 00:40:10.950
- It's really cool that your
book was built R Markdown as well.

00:40:10.950 --> 00:40:15.450
I was wondering, what was that
process like of writing a book?

00:40:15.450 --> 00:40:21.090
And how did you decide that
you were ready to write a book?

00:40:21.090 --> 00:40:22.290
- Two things. One,

00:40:22.290 --> 00:40:25.890
I'll start off by saying that
if it were not R Markdown,

00:40:25.890 --> 00:40:28.870
there's no way I ever
would've written that thing.

00:40:28.870 --> 00:40:31.740
I can't imagine-- certainly,
I'm not going to write

00:40:31.740 --> 00:40:34.380
the entire document in lawtech.

00:40:34.380 --> 00:40:36.420
There are people who
can, and God bless them.

00:40:36.420 --> 00:40:39.570
I'm not the sort of
person that could have

00:40:39.570 --> 00:40:41.940
that much lawtech [LAUGHS]

00:40:41.940 --> 00:40:43.950
to think about.

00:40:43.950 --> 00:40:47.040
Nor do I want to juggle about

00:40:47.040 --> 00:40:50.940
12 or 13 different Word
documents or one monolithic

00:40:50.940 --> 00:40:55.620
Word document. So R Markdown
was a tremendous solution. And

00:40:55.620 --> 00:40:58.065
it was really empowering.

00:40:58.065 --> 00:40:59.220
The

00:40:59.220 --> 00:41:02.490
idea for the book
came about from

00:41:02.490 --> 00:41:07.680
workshops that I and others
had been doing for the CAS.

00:41:07.680 --> 00:41:11.580
We had 1-day workshops,
day and a half workshops.

00:41:11.580 --> 00:41:18.680
And I found that there was
a lot of foundational material, thinking about

00:41:18.680 --> 00:41:23.490
vectors, and slicing vectors,
and concatenation, and list,

00:41:23.490 --> 00:41:30.060
and data frames. This was taking
up a large amount of scarce time.

00:41:30.060 --> 00:41:33.840
Teach somebody to be an actuary
using R in about 7 and 1/2

00:41:33.840 --> 00:41:35.670
hours. That's a tall order.

00:41:35.670 --> 00:41:39.450
And when you spend about 3
hours or more just making sure

00:41:39.450 --> 00:41:41.580
that folks understand
what a data frame is,

00:41:41.580 --> 00:41:44.175
that scares time
that we're losing.

00:41:44.175 --> 00:41:46.920
So those early slide decks,

00:41:46.920 --> 00:41:49.020
probably all of which were
written in Markdown, by the

00:41:49.020 --> 00:41:53.410
way, then translating to Reveal
JS or something like that,

00:41:53.410 --> 00:41:58.350
we already had all of the
script for it for those. And I

00:41:58.350 --> 00:42:00.820
sat down with those
decks and said, OK,

00:42:00.820 --> 00:42:02.200
these look like book chapters.

00:42:02.200 --> 00:42:04.980
So you take at
45-minute session.

00:42:04.980 --> 00:42:07.740
And you've already got a lot
of the code in the examples.

00:42:07.740 --> 00:42:10.000
And then, at the
time, I thought,

00:42:10.000 --> 00:42:12.930
I'll just flesh this out with
a few paragraphs and, bang,

00:42:12.930 --> 00:42:17.100
I'll have a book. It wound
up being more complicated than that.

00:42:17.100 --> 00:42:20.010
It turns out that

00:42:20.010 --> 00:42:23.580
it does take a little bit of
time to write all of the words

00:42:23.580 --> 00:42:25.930
in such a way that
they make sense.

00:42:25.930 --> 00:42:27.300
But at that point,
it was too late.

00:42:27.300 --> 00:42:30.660
I was already I think
80 pages into it and

00:42:30.660 --> 00:42:32.250
just needed to finish.

00:42:32.250 --> 00:42:35.130
So that's kind of a story
about how that got started.

00:42:35.130 --> 00:42:38.400
We started with some R Markdown

00:42:38.400 --> 00:42:40.400
files.

00:42:41.350 --> 00:42:44.320
The need for it is to give
folks some foundational

00:42:44.320 --> 00:42:47.140
knowledge. And it just
kind of grew from there.

00:42:49.840 --> 00:42:53.020
- Awesome. Thank you.

00:42:53.020 --> 00:42:55.990
One other anonymous
question that came in--

00:42:55.990 --> 00:42:58.540
and thank you to the team
gathering those for me in the

00:42:58.540 --> 00:43:02.120
background-- was, "How do you
share your work currently,

00:43:02.120 --> 00:43:06.250
especially when teams don't have R
and need to upload CSV files?"

00:43:08.830 --> 00:43:11.690
- I don't have a real
strong need to do that.

00:43:11.690 --> 00:43:15.940
So my sharing use case is
probably not going to resemble yours.

00:43:15.940 --> 00:43:20.650
Because I'm guessing that the question
comes from some points of friction.

00:43:20.650 --> 00:43:22.650
What I've done

00:43:23.920 --> 00:43:27.640
is I'm going to
say SharePoints.

00:43:27.640 --> 00:43:30.700
Grad school project,
I'm near the end

00:43:30.700 --> 00:43:32.260
of a master's degree.

00:43:32.260 --> 00:43:34.630
And so I had had some
grad school projects.

00:43:34.630 --> 00:43:36.760
It's Google Drive.

00:43:36.760 --> 00:43:38.470
The kids, they love Google.

00:43:38.470 --> 00:43:42.280
Whereas the older corporate
people like SharePoint.

00:43:42.280 --> 00:43:44.810
They're both fine.

00:43:44.810 --> 00:43:48.700
That's what I use to share
data, like CSV. Files.

00:43:48.700 --> 00:43:51.550
What I love to do
is to collaborate on GitHub.

00:43:51.550 --> 00:43:54.790
Because it is controlled.

00:43:54.790 --> 00:43:56.725
It feels safe

00:43:56.725 --> 00:43:58.810
and deliberate.

00:43:58.810 --> 00:44:00.670
There are obviously

00:44:00.670 --> 00:44:04.420
some frictional points
there as well. But

00:44:04.420 --> 00:44:07.540
beyond that, I'm curious
what other folks use,

00:44:07.540 --> 00:44:09.730
if there is some interesting
magic bullet out there.

00:44:11.730 --> 00:44:14.250
- Yeah. Any thoughts,

00:44:14.250 --> 00:44:16.230
feel free to just
unmute-- I can watch--

00:44:16.230 --> 00:44:18.920
or raise your hand here.

00:44:18.920 --> 00:44:23.780
I know one way that came
up previously in a Hangout was

00:44:23.780 --> 00:44:27.260
creating a shiny
application where

00:44:27.260 --> 00:44:29.930
people who didn't
use R could upload

00:44:29.930 --> 00:44:33.380
the data set and work
with it in the app.

00:44:33.380 --> 00:44:37.010
But, yeah, any other ideas, feel
free to share in the chat too.

00:44:40.960 --> 00:44:42.040
Another question,

00:44:42.040 --> 00:44:44.350
I actually meant to ask
you this in the beginning.

00:44:44.350 --> 00:44:47.320
But thinking about the projects
that you've been working on

00:44:47.320 --> 00:44:51.880
lately and thinking into maybe the
next year ahead for your team,

00:44:51.880 --> 00:44:54.010
what's something that
you're really excited about?

00:44:56.170 --> 00:44:57.220
- Me, or others.

00:44:57.220 --> 00:44:58.480
RACHAEL DEMPSEY:
You. You, yeah.

00:44:58.480 --> 00:45:00.480
- Oh, gosh, what am
I excited-- oh, my.

00:45:03.830 --> 00:45:06.110
I'm caught off guard by that.

00:45:06.110 --> 00:45:08.900
My immediate response is
kind of disheartening.

00:45:08.900 --> 00:45:11.030
I don't know that I'm
excited about anything.

00:45:11.030 --> 00:45:13.675
I'll say this.

00:45:13.675 --> 00:45:18.275
I'm excited about my next
trip to a new country

00:45:18.275 --> 00:45:20.390
to talk about R.

00:45:20.390 --> 00:45:22.310
I'm cheating a little bit here.

00:45:22.310 --> 00:45:26.390
I went to South America for
the first time last year. I was

00:45:26.390 --> 00:45:29.810
invited to give a
1-day workshop on

00:45:29.810 --> 00:45:34.400
statistical learning in Bogota,
Colombia. That was fantastic,

00:45:34.400 --> 00:45:38.960
similar to the experience that
I had going to Germany and

00:45:38.960 --> 00:45:42.920
seeing a fresh perspective
on the work that I was doing.

00:45:42.920 --> 00:45:46.220
Ditto down there.

00:45:46.220 --> 00:45:51.080
The folks there were just
super motivated. And they had

00:45:51.080 --> 00:45:55.580
a really interesting way of
looking at things. And I enjoyed

00:45:55.580 --> 00:45:58.760
having the opportunity to
interact with other folks.

00:45:58.760 --> 00:46:00.680
It's been a little while
now, but I taught --

00:46:00.680 --> 00:46:04.820
I co-instructed a short
course on R in Rwanda.

00:46:04.820 --> 00:46:05.960
That was fun.

00:46:05.960 --> 00:46:07.100
So

00:46:07.100 --> 00:46:08.990
nothing like that on the radar.

00:46:08.990 --> 00:46:11.280
So I can't really claim
to be excited about that.

00:46:11.280 --> 00:46:13.880
But if anybody's outside
of the United States,

00:46:13.880 --> 00:46:16.490
particularly South
America or Africa,

00:46:16.490 --> 00:46:19.130
and you want me to--
I'll pay my own airfare.

00:46:19.130 --> 00:46:23.210
But if you'll give me an audience
for a couple of days or a week,

00:46:23.210 --> 00:46:27.110
I'd love to go teach R to
anybody that wants to be taught by me,

00:46:29.320 --> 00:46:29.485
-

00:46:29.485 --> 00:46:31.810
What's the best place for
people to follow up with you on

00:46:31.810 --> 00:46:33.950
that? Is it LinkedIn?

00:46:33.950 --> 00:46:35.320
- That's a good place, yeah.

00:46:35.320 --> 00:46:37.300
And feel free to
email me directly.

00:46:37.300 --> 00:46:40.615
I'm going to give you my
workplace email in the chat.

00:46:43.660 --> 00:46:45.130
So there's my
professional place.

00:46:45.130 --> 00:46:47.260
And then you can also find me--

00:46:47.260 --> 00:46:51.970
