00:46:59.800 --> 00:47:04.854
years ago, that I'm just kind
of sticking with for now.

00:47:04.854 --> 00:47:07.330
- Awesome. Thank you.

00:47:07.330 --> 00:47:10.135
Another anonymous question
I saw that came in is,

00:47:10.135 --> 00:47:14.170
"Is there much of an
actuarial R community, for example, folks

00:47:14.170 --> 00:47:17.290
collaborating on common
packages with developers

00:47:17.290 --> 00:47:21.680
between firms or sharing use
cases and best practices?

00:47:21.680 --> 00:47:23.140
- So

00:47:23.140 --> 00:47:25.870
I think there is
not yet a community,

00:47:25.870 --> 00:47:29.290
though we're actively
working to create one.

00:47:29.290 --> 00:47:31.290
There is a clique.

00:47:32.435 --> 00:47:35.050
There is a small
group of people.

00:47:35.050 --> 00:47:38.125
Every 2 weeks, there's

00:47:38.125 --> 00:47:42.190
a meetup of some folks,
a virtual meetup,

00:47:42.190 --> 00:47:46.720
of actuaries, who are using
open source, generally. We got

00:47:46.720 --> 00:47:49.420
some Python folks
on there as well.

00:47:49.420 --> 00:47:54.310
So there's a clique,
but not yet a community.

00:47:54.310 --> 00:47:54.395
-

00:47:54.395 --> 00:47:59.220
How would we find out about
this clique if we were interested?

00:47:59.220 --> 00:48:02.160
- Good question. I hate
to sound kind of gatekeepy,

00:48:02.160 --> 00:48:05.220
but I think you might
have to be an actuary.

00:48:05.220 --> 00:48:06.780
- Oh, yes, I mean,

00:48:06.780 --> 00:48:09.750
if somebody is an actuary
that wants to join.

00:48:09.750 --> 00:48:10.560
- Send me an email.

00:48:10.560 --> 00:48:11.020
RACHAEL DEMPSEY: OK.

00:48:11.020 --> 00:48:11.940
- Send me an e-mail.

00:48:11.940 --> 00:48:14.340
And send me an email and
have a bit of patience.

00:48:14.340 --> 00:48:16.980
I get a lot of email.

00:48:16.980 --> 00:48:21.270
And my response rate is not
what I would like it to be.

00:48:21.270 --> 00:48:24.390
Trust me, I really want to
respond, but I get a lot of

00:48:24.390 --> 00:48:28.020
email. But please, please, please,
reach out to me, or

00:48:28.020 --> 00:48:31.620
find me on LinkedIn as well.

00:48:31.620 --> 00:48:36.170
Having a space for
actuaries and non-actuaries

00:48:36.170 --> 00:48:39.120
to connect, that's actually
a really good thing,

00:48:39.120 --> 00:48:41.070
that that's a good idea.

00:48:41.070 --> 00:48:45.250
And I'd love to work with
any non actuaries to make that happen,

00:48:45.250 --> 00:48:47.070
if anybody wants to
hit me up about that.

00:48:47.070 --> 00:48:50.460
I'm glancing at Adam and
my gallery [INAUDIBLE].

00:48:50.460 --> 00:48:52.680
- [CHUCKLES]

00:48:52.680 --> 00:48:53.610
Sounds like Javier has a--

00:48:53.610 --> 00:48:54.060
JAVIER: It's talk.

00:48:54.060 --> 00:48:56.850
[LAUGHTER]

00:48:56.850 --> 00:48:56.865
-

00:48:56.865 --> 00:48:58.830
I was going to say sounds
like having your has a lot of

00:48:58.830 --> 00:49:02.310
experience working
with actuaries as well.

00:49:02.310 --> 00:49:04.920
- Yeah, great, great, yeah,

00:49:04.920 --> 00:49:07.570
good to meet you both.

00:49:07.570 --> 00:49:08.950
- OK,

00:49:08.950 --> 00:49:11.380
Bill had a question in the
chat. And I'm going to read it,

00:49:11.380 --> 00:49:15.370
bill, because I don't think
you are unmuting here. But

00:49:15.370 --> 00:49:17.560
the question iet--

00:49:17.560 --> 00:49:19.420
let me summarize a little bit--

00:49:19.420 --> 00:49:22.630
do you have any opinion
on using ChatGPT

00:49:22.630 --> 00:49:24.850
and the effects
on your industry?

00:49:28.400 --> 00:49:30.548
- Not yet.

00:49:30.548 --> 00:49:35.150
The only thing that I know--
I know very little about ChatGPT.

00:49:35.150 --> 00:49:39.770
I want to be more conversant
in AI. A few years

00:49:39.770 --> 00:49:42.590
back, I just kind of dabbled

00:49:42.590 --> 00:49:45.560
with just some very,
very basic exercises ,

00:49:45.560 --> 00:49:50.030
just to understand
the mechanical flow.

00:49:50.030 --> 00:49:51.290
I'm blanking on the co-author,

00:49:51.290 --> 00:49:54.470
but JJ Allaire has
a really good book

00:49:54.470 --> 00:49:58.580
about AI that was kind
of my intro to that.

00:49:58.580 --> 00:50:01.056
AI, generally, is--

00:50:04.460 --> 00:50:06.470
how do I phrase this?

00:50:06.470 --> 00:50:10.520
It's a slightly awkward
fit for actuarial practice.

00:50:10.520 --> 00:50:13.560
And that's my view,
and it may be wrong.

00:50:13.560 --> 00:50:16.490
There are definitely
cases where it works.

00:50:16.490 --> 00:50:22.190
My sense is that AI relies
on a lot of observations.

00:50:22.190 --> 00:50:25.440
Insurance claims,
thankfully, are pretty rare.

00:50:25.440 --> 00:50:29.360
So those observations are
kind of hard to come by.

00:50:29.360 --> 00:50:29.750
Now,

00:50:29.750 --> 00:50:32.840
that's not to say that it has
no place whatsoever in the

00:50:32.840 --> 00:50:33.860
actuarial workflow.

00:50:33.860 --> 00:50:37.100
And we've got some papers
that have looked at this.

00:50:37.100 --> 00:50:38.270
Last year, at a conference,

00:50:38.270 --> 00:50:44.141
we had a really good
talk about long, short-term memory models

00:50:44.141 --> 00:50:49.490
in loss reserving. So that's
an active conversation. And we're

00:50:49.490 --> 00:50:52.130
looking at more and more
applications. Ron Richmond--

00:50:52.130 --> 00:50:54.840
I'll throw his name
out there-- Is a very,

00:50:54.840 --> 00:50:58.400
very talented actuary who's
looking at AI and deep learning

00:50:58.400 --> 00:51:00.170
in actuarial applications.

00:51:00.170 --> 00:51:02.960
We're turning to ChatGPT.

00:51:02.960 --> 00:51:05.240
I've already outed
myself as an old man.

00:51:05.240 --> 00:51:08.990
And I haven't really used
it very much, other than,

00:51:08.990 --> 00:51:10.970
I think it was last
week or the week before,

00:51:10.970 --> 00:51:12.950
I posted something on LinkedIn.

00:51:12.950 --> 00:51:16.520
Someone had posed a
question to ChatGPT about

00:51:16.520 --> 00:51:17.810
"The Grateful Dead."

00:51:17.810 --> 00:51:19.700
I'm a big Deadhead.

00:51:19.700 --> 00:51:21.700
And the answer was just wrong.

00:51:24.440 --> 00:51:26.330
So

00:51:26.330 --> 00:51:29.690
I know that some actuaries have
fed an actuarial exam to see if

00:51:29.690 --> 00:51:34.280
it can pass. And so far,
it can't. So that's good news for

00:51:34.280 --> 00:51:37.430
the profession, I suppose. But--

00:51:37.430 --> 00:51:40.130
- Hey, Brian, that's
called hallucinating. As a Deadhead,

00:51:40.130 --> 00:51:43.130
you should be familiar
with that concept.

00:51:43.130 --> 00:51:45.530
- Well, OK.

00:51:45.530 --> 00:51:49.175
I'm a Deadhead, but I'm
also a big database nerd.

00:51:49.175 --> 00:51:50.870
And don't get me started

00:51:50.870 --> 00:51:55.970
on the statistical analysis
of "Grateful Dead" performances.

00:51:55.970 --> 00:51:57.275
But

00:51:57.275 --> 00:51:59.840
at the moment,
especially, specifically something

00:51:59.840 --> 00:52:02.470
like ChatGPT,

00:52:02.470 --> 00:52:04.970
there are not active
applications that I'm aware of.

00:52:04.970 --> 00:52:07.400
Deep learning generally, yeah

00:52:07.400 --> 00:52:08.780
there are a few.

00:52:08.780 --> 00:52:10.310
But

00:52:10.310 --> 00:52:16.160
I see a lot more scope for
lift for actuaries out of Bayesian techniques.

00:52:16.160 --> 00:52:18.160
Because

00:52:18.930 --> 00:52:21.180
we simply don't have
a lot of observations.

00:52:21.180 --> 00:52:26.880
But we have loads of qualitative

00:52:26.880 --> 00:52:29.130
wisdom to bring to bear.

00:52:29.130 --> 00:52:32.190
And I would say that
Bayesian techniques are

00:52:32.190 --> 00:52:35.370
just woefully underutilized.

00:52:35.370 --> 00:52:38.190
Actuaries were at the
forefront of promoting Bayesian

00:52:38.190 --> 00:52:41.160
techniques for a long time.
There's a really good book

00:52:41.160 --> 00:52:43.890
called, "The Theory
That Wouldn't Die."

00:52:43.890 --> 00:52:47.190
Similar to "The Lady Tasting
Tea," it's kind of a popular,

00:52:47.190 --> 00:52:50.145
easy-ish to read book
about statistics.

00:52:50.145 --> 00:52:53.820
There is an entire
chapter about actuaries,

00:52:53.820 --> 00:52:57.030
one of the few books you're
going to find that will do that.

00:52:59.660 --> 00:53:01.470
But they're just
not mainstream.

00:53:01.470 --> 00:53:03.500
And especially
with the advances

00:53:03.500 --> 00:53:05.450
in something like MCMC,

00:53:05.450 --> 00:53:07.230
there's no reason why
we can't be using them.

00:53:07.230 --> 00:53:10.910
So I'm loads more bullish
on Bayesian techniques than I am

00:53:10.910 --> 00:53:13.670
on deep learning, not immune

00:53:13.670 --> 00:53:15.770
to deep learning. But that
is something that we're looking

00:53:15.770 --> 00:53:20.800
at. But I see a lot
more lift out of Bayesian techniques.

00:53:20.800 --> 00:53:23.800
- Just to confirm the book
that you mentioned by JJ Allaire,

00:53:23.800 --> 00:53:26.560
is that the "Deep
Learning With R" book?

00:53:26.560 --> 00:53:29.180
- Probably. He had
a co-author on that?

00:53:29.180 --> 00:53:31.720
- Yep, worked with Francois

00:53:31.720 --> 00:53:35.290
and Tomassz. Let me
put that into the

00:53:35.290 --> 00:53:39.910
chat again here. Because I
actually just learned this week that

00:53:39.910 --> 00:53:43.600
they updated this book.
So there's a second edition.

00:53:43.600 --> 00:53:44.260
- Oh, great.

00:53:44.260 --> 00:53:46.330
RACHAEL DEMPSEY: We had
some new ones in the office.

00:53:46.330 --> 00:53:51.380
And I asked JJ to sign one
the other day for the AstraZeneca conference.

00:53:51.380 --> 00:53:52.345
- OK.

00:53:52.345 --> 00:53:54.235
That's a really fantastic book.

00:53:54.235 --> 00:53:56.235
I'll need to pick up
the second edition.

00:53:59.182 --> 00:53:59.680
RACHAEL DEMPSEY: Awesome.

00:53:59.680 --> 00:54:02.410
Let me just double
check here whether-- oh,

00:54:02.410 --> 00:54:05.140
I see a few new questions here.

00:54:05.140 --> 00:54:05.710
I was going to say,

00:54:05.710 --> 00:54:08.890
if there's questions I have
missed in the last remaining 5

00:54:08.890 --> 00:54:13.030
minutes or so here, feel free
to just raise your hand on Zoom.

00:54:13.030 --> 00:54:16.030
But, Santiago, I see
one in the chat here.

00:54:18.680 --> 00:54:20.990
SANTIAGO: It wasn't, like,
an actual question. But

00:54:20.990 --> 00:54:24.810
on the conversation of GenAI
and foundational models--

00:54:24.810 --> 00:54:26.910
I'm sure many others have
gotten this question.

00:54:26.910 --> 00:54:29.990
We've been asked to look
for applications of these things

00:54:29.990 --> 00:54:34.370
and how can we start
applying them internally?

00:54:34.370 --> 00:54:36.260
I don't think they realize

00:54:36.260 --> 00:54:39.480
applications are
fewer and far between.

00:54:39.480 --> 00:54:40.940
But it depends. I don't know.

00:54:40.940 --> 00:54:43.790
One of my proposals was,

00:54:43.790 --> 00:54:46.115
as a consulting firm,
they're very big on brand.

00:54:46.115 --> 00:54:48.650
I'm sure others have
to deal with that too,

00:54:48.650 --> 00:54:51.380
what to capitalize,
what not to capitalize.

00:54:51.380 --> 00:54:53.480
Why not train

00:54:53.480 --> 00:54:56.450
something, like
Falcon or ChatGPT,

00:54:56.450 --> 00:54:58.670
on your brand guide

00:54:58.670 --> 00:55:00.110
so that you don't
have to read it.

00:55:00.110 --> 00:55:03.950
You just pass it your
documents, and it would auto-brand

00:55:03.950 --> 00:55:07.140
fix things for you.

00:55:07.140 --> 00:55:08.990
It was kind of, like, a,
I don't know, a sarcastic

00:55:08.990 --> 00:55:11.270
response. But I feel like
there would be application for that.

00:55:13.790 --> 00:55:17.980
- Sarcastic or
not, it sounds like

00:55:17.980 --> 00:55:21.000
a decent place for it.

00:55:21.000 --> 00:55:23.870
I think there is a
lot of automatable

00:55:25.870 --> 00:55:28.450
activity that ChatGPT

00:55:28.450 --> 00:55:30.580
can help with.

00:55:30.580 --> 00:55:34.180
- I love that example, Santiago.

00:55:34.180 --> 00:55:35.890
I know we've talked
about this before here.

00:55:35.890 --> 00:55:40.330
But I always think the best
way to make your proof of concept

00:55:40.330 --> 00:55:43.210
or something that you share
with your leadership team stand

00:55:43.210 --> 00:55:47.860
out is to actually use
your company brand in-- I don't

00:55:47.860 --> 00:55:49.450
know, if it was a Shiny app,

00:55:49.450 --> 00:55:53.230
using your logo and
the company colors.

00:55:53.230 --> 00:55:56.680
Which I know Javier had
wrote in a blog post before,

00:55:56.680 --> 00:55:59.890
would be cool to see if
he could run that through ChatGPT.

00:56:01.960 --> 00:56:04.030
Let's see, any

00:56:04.030 --> 00:56:09.310
other questions that I
may have missed here?

00:56:09.310 --> 00:56:11.280
Let's see.

00:56:11.280 --> 00:56:13.310
A question

00:56:13.310 --> 00:56:16.370
I love to ask Brian is,

00:56:16.370 --> 00:56:22.370
thinking about your career so
far and your mentors along the

00:56:22.370 --> 00:56:23.630
way throughout your career,

00:56:23.630 --> 00:56:27.230
is there a piece of career
advice that stands out to you?

00:56:30.490 --> 00:56:32.490
- Good question.

00:56:33.280 --> 00:56:35.560
I'm nor sure that I'm an
amazing person to give career

00:56:35.560 --> 00:56:39.490
advice. I had a lot of
luck over the years. But

00:56:39.490 --> 00:56:41.530
I've kind of

00:56:41.530 --> 00:56:43.960
randomly gone from
one thing to the next.

00:56:43.960 --> 00:56:47.550
And I don't know that
that's great advice.

00:56:47.550 --> 00:56:52.990
Or maybe I could spin that
positively and just say

00:56:52.990 --> 00:56:57.085
I feel like I benefited
from an openness

00:56:57.085 --> 00:57:01.120
to do other things, and
also just an awareness that,

00:57:01.120 --> 00:57:02.770
depending on what
industry you're in,

00:57:02.770 --> 00:57:04.720
you will need some resilience.

00:57:04.720 --> 00:57:06.100
I worked for a really cool,

00:57:06.100 --> 00:57:09.980
really fun startup for
about a year, until

00:57:09.980 --> 00:57:12.120
it just evaporated. Like,

00:57:12.120 --> 00:57:14.890
you go into work one day and
they say, well, you know, it's been fun,

00:57:14.890 --> 00:57:18.310
but the market has other
ideas about the viability of what

00:57:18.310 --> 00:57:19.840
we're all doing here.

00:57:19.840 --> 00:57:22.570
So you do need some
resiliency and some openness.

00:57:24.670 --> 00:57:27.850
And I guess the mentor there
has just been experience,

00:57:27.850 --> 00:57:30.610
just having lived through

00:57:30.610 --> 00:57:32.080
some good fortune

00:57:32.080 --> 00:57:36.940
and some not so good episodes, has been

00:57:36.940 --> 00:57:39.010
what has taught me that.

00:57:39.010 --> 00:57:41.570
I will say, though,
that I've got a really,

00:57:41.570 --> 00:57:43.970
really cool network. I've
worked with some really,

00:57:43.970 --> 00:57:46.660
really great people.

00:57:46.660 --> 00:57:47.380
This is advice.

00:57:47.380 --> 00:57:51.640
I'm not going to be the
first person to have shared this. But

00:57:51.640 --> 00:57:52.930
as often as you can,

00:57:52.930 --> 00:57:56.950
try to make sure that you're
the dumbest person at the table,

00:57:56.950 --> 00:57:59.200
not so hard for me. But

00:57:59.200 --> 00:58:01.830
being deliberate about that

00:58:01.830 --> 00:58:03.830
has been useful.

00:58:06.500 --> 00:58:08.650
- I appreciate that advice.

00:58:08.650 --> 00:58:09.810
And

00:58:09.810 --> 00:58:12.700
I also think,

00:58:12.700 --> 00:58:14.830
listening to you today,

00:58:14.830 --> 00:58:18.430
what I've learned is to also
ask for things that you want. Like,

00:58:18.430 --> 00:58:23.560
if you want to go and travel to
a different country and teach a workshop there,

00:58:23.560 --> 00:58:28.360
ask people [CHUCKLES] if--
If they're looking for that.

00:58:28.360 --> 00:58:31.840
Brian, thank you so much
for joining us today and sharing

00:58:31.840 --> 00:58:34.240
your experience.
This has been great.

00:58:34.240 --> 00:58:37.210
I'm going to share your
LinkedIn here in the chat for

00:58:37.210 --> 00:58:39.210
everybody as well.

00:58:40.530 --> 00:58:43.520
But thank you all for
the great questions too.

00:58:43.520 --> 00:58:45.510
Have a great rest of
the day, everybody.

